    "q-64444": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64444",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t),$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+2y\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L\\{y\\}=Y(s).$ \n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}L\\{y\\}-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s).\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{({s}^{2}+1)({s}^{2}+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
