    "q-64457": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64457",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+y\\} & =L\\{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+3L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =3L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\}$ and $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$  we use formulas for the Laplace transforms of the derivatives and apply the initial conditions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\} & =sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)+1 \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\} & ={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)+s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+3L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =3L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)+s)+3(sY(s)+1)+Y(s) & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+3s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+1}-s-3 \\\\ ({s}^{2}+3s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{3-{s}^{3}-s-3{s}^{2}-3}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+3s+1)Y(s) & =-\\frac{s({s}^{2}+3s+1)}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
