    "q-345085": {
      "dom_id": "step-q345085",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)-y(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)-3-Y(s)=-6(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-3-Y(s) & =-6(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}) \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =3-\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =\\frac{3({s}^{2}-1)}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>1.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+1}\\} \\\\ y(t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
